{"adjusted_val_bpb": -1.7491792230269352, "attempt": 0, "charged": true, "critic": {"issues": ["The edit removes the wall constraints (c-r >= 0 and 1-c-r >= 0) from cons and replaces them with simple bounds on the variables: centres in [0,1] and radii in [0,0.5]. Those bounds do not keep each circle inside the square. A circle can sit at the edge with any radius up to 0.5, so SLSQP's optimum breaks the walls. The repair step then has to shrink radii a lot, which explains the collapse from about 2.94 to 1.7492. This is a real modelling error in the candidate, but the harness still measured it correctly: the packing it produced is valid and simply bad. So no veto. A correct version would keep the r >= 0 constraint as a bound and keep the four wall inequalities in cons.", "The harness checked validity and set no integrity flags. I found no file access, no pasted coordinates and no change to the use of the seed.", "z recomputed: (-1.7492 - (-2.9366)) / (0.0034*sqrt(1/1 + 1/2)) = 1.1874/0.004164 \u2248 285.2 in size. With the harness's sign convention (negative means worse) that is about -285. The harness reports -282.44, a small gap that probably comes from a slightly different estimate of sd. Either way the decision to reject stands.", "Only one candidate run was used. That is fine here, because the gap is hundreds of noise sd."], "ok": true, "veto": false}, "decision": "reject", "description": "Move the wall and radius-nonnegativity constraints into SLSQP variable bounds (centres in [0,1], radii in [0,0.5]), leaving only pair-overlap constraints, with _repair still enforcing validity.", "edit_class": "structural", "flags": [], "gpu": "cherry", "idea_id": "b031", "incumbent_after": {"mean": -2.936576587469623, "n": 2}, "incumbent_before": {"mean": -2.936576587469623, "n": 2}, "job_id": "B2_p32_SS_3_s0-candidate-9_1795058141539", "kind": "candidate", "lint": {"hard": [], "ok": true, "warnings": []}, "model_calls": [{"cost_usd_nominal": 0.00197173, "cost_usd_plan": 0.001